<article class="env-lemma" id="0625">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0625">Lemma <span data-tag="0625">15.29.4</span>.</a> Let $f_1, \ldots , f_ r \in R$ be a sequence. Let $(x_{ij})$ be an invertible $r \times r$-matrix with coefficients in $R$. Then the complexes $K_\bullet (f_\bullet )$ and </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  K_\bullet (\sum x_{1j}f_ j, \sum x_{2j}f_ j, \ldots , \sum x_{rj}f_ j)  \]
</div>
  <p> are isomorphic. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Set $g_ i = \sum x_{ij}f_ j$. The matrix $(x_{ji})$ gives an isomorphism $x : R^{\oplus r} \to R^{\oplus r}$ such that $(g_1, \ldots , g_ r) = (f_1, \ldots , f_ r) \circ x$. Hence this follows from the functoriality of the Koszul complex described in Lemma <a href="/tag/0624" data-tag="0624">15.29.3</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>